mixin () {
  public query func getApiDoc() : async Text {
    "# QRNova Backend API\n\n## Purpose\n\nQRNova is a free, browser-side QR code generator. All QR code generation, customization, and image export happen entirely in the user's browser. The backend canister therefore stores **no QR content and no user records**; it exists only to provide the shared authorization surface and the OQL exposure mixin required by the platform.\n\n## Public methods\n\n- `getApiDoc() : async Text` (query) — returns this document. It is a static, read-only query that reads no canister state and can be called by anyone, including anonymous callers.\n- `_initialize_access_control()` — provided by the shared authorization mixin. Registers the calling principal. The first caller to invoke it becomes the initial administrator; subsequent callers receive the default (non-admin) role. See the authorization section below.\n- `getUserRole()`, `assignRole()`, `isAdmin()`, `hasPermission()` — role-management and role-query methods provided by the shared authorization mixin.\n- `schema()` and `execute()` — provided by the OQL `Expose` mixin. QRNova registers **no entities** (`entities = []`), so `schema()` reports an empty entity list and `execute()` has nothing to query. These methods exist only because the platform requires the Expose mixin to be included.\n\n## Authentication and authorization\n\n- `getApiDoc()` requires no authentication and is safe for anonymous callers.\n- The authorization surface is preserved for future optional features. QRNova's core QR generation, customization, and downloads never require an account, a subscription, or a signed-in identity.\n- The app's frontend pins an Internet Identity derivation origin, published at `/.well-known/ii-derivation-origin` when available. An agent already holding the user's Internet Identity authorization derives the correct per-app principal against that origin (for example `icp identity link web <name> --app <host>`). Such a delegation acts with the user's full authority in this app until it expires.\n- Registration is a prerequisite for any role-guarded call. A direct API caller registers by calling `_initialize_access_control` once as a signed-in caller before any role-guarded call, guarded queries included. The first initializer receives the administrator role; every subsequent caller receives the default non-admin role. An unregistered or anonymous caller receives the authorization mixin's rejection (a trap) on guarded endpoints. A caller can be unregistered even when it belongs to the app's owner: registration happens only when a caller signs in through the app's own frontend, and a signed-in caller derived against a different origin is a different principal than the one the frontend registered.\n\n## Units and encoding\n\n- All method arguments and return values use Candid encoding over the Internet Computer's standard interface.\n- `getApiDoc()` returns a UTF-8 `Text` value containing Markdown.\n- No timestamps, blobs, or numeric units are exposed by this backend, because it stores no application data.\n\n## Lifecycle and polling\n\n- `getApiDoc()` is a pure query: it has no lifecycle, no state transitions, and no completion condition. It may be called any number of times and always returns the same static document.\n- No polling is required or useful for any QRNova backend method.\n\n## Mutation retry safety\n\n- `getApiDoc()` is read-only and idempotent; retrying it is always safe.\n- `_initialize_access_control()` is idempotent with respect to an already-registered caller: calling it again does not change that caller's role. It is **not** idempotent across different callers — the first caller to invoke it becomes the administrator, so it must be called exactly once by the intended administrator.\n- `assignRole()` is a destructive mutation of another principal's role and is not idempotent in effect; retrying it re-applies the same role assignment.\n\n## Errors, limits, and gotchas\n\n- `getApiDoc()` never traps and has no input, so it cannot fail on caller input.\n- Role-guarded methods trap for unregistered or insufficiently privileged callers; the trap message is produced by the shared authorization mixin.\n- `schema()` returns an empty entity list and `execute()` returns no rows: QRNova intentionally exposes no queryable data. Do not expect QR codes or user data to be retrievable through the backend — they never leave the browser.\n"
  };
};
